== Part A: the disagreement rate bounds beta for every human ==
3000 random instances (stochastic humans; kernels and history-dependent answerers):
  smallest value of L*rho_dis - beta(A)          = -4.441e-16   (claim: >= 0)
  largest value of  L*rho_dis - beta(A) - L*s_P  = 4.441e-16   (claim: <= 0)

== Part B1: a fair coin against a random function; each input asked once per round ==
L = 1, N inputs drawn uniformly, A = fair coin: beta = 0 against the coin, 1/2 against every function.
  N=     10000 m=   10: exact TV between the two laws of the validation transcript = 2.248e-03   (m(m-1)/(2N) = 4.500e-03)
  N=     10000 m=   50: exact TV between the two laws of the validation transcript = 5.941e-02   (m(m-1)/(2N) = 1.225e-01)
  N=   1000000 m=  100: exact TV between the two laws of the validation transcript = 2.472e-03   (m(m-1)/(2N) = 4.950e-03)
  N=   1000000 m= 1000: exact TV between the two laws of the validation transcript = 2.210e-01   (m(m-1)/(2N) = 4.995e-01)
  N=1000000000 m= 1000: exact TV between the two laws of the validation transcript = 2.497e-04   (m(m-1)/(2N) = 4.995e-04)
  N=1000000000 m=10000: TV <= m(m-1)/(2N) = 4.999e-02
  A procedure that certifies 'beta(A) < 1/2' with probability c when the human is the coin
  certifies it with average probability at least c - TV when the human is a function, where it is false.
== Part B2: uniform on Y against uniform on a random half of Y; questions may be repeated ==
  |Y|=    16 r=  2 answers at one input: exact TV = 6.250e-02   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 6.250e-02)
  |Y|=    16 r=  4 answers at one input: exact TV = 2.563e-01   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 3.750e-01)
  |Y|=   256 r=  4 answers at one input: exact TV = 2.294e-02   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 2.344e-02)
  |Y|=   256 r= 16 answers at one input: exact TV = 2.436e-01   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 4.688e-01)
  |Y|= 65536 r= 16 answers at one input: exact TV = 1.826e-03   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 1.831e-03)
  |Y|= 65536 r= 64 answers at one input: exact TV = 2.940e-02   (r(r-1)/(2M) = 3.076e-02)
  For m questions in all, at any inputs: TV <= m(m-1)/(2|Y|).  With answers of 100 bits and m = 10^9: 3.9e-13

== Part C: re-asking certifies beta for a small answer set, with no assumption A2 ==
  m=200 rounds x r=100 repeats, A = P (beta = 0): beta/L = 0.0000; mean certified bound = 0.1648; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  m=200 rounds x r=100 repeats, A shifted by 0.1 at every input: beta/L = 0.1000; mean certified bound = 0.2266; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  m=200 rounds x r=100 repeats, A deterministic (coins filled in): beta/L = 0.4024; mean certified bound = 0.5283; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  m=400 rounds x r=400 repeats, A = P (beta = 0): beta/L = 0.0000; mean certified bound = 0.0981; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  m=400 rounds x r=400 repeats, A shifted by 0.1 at every input: beta/L = 0.1000; mean certified bound = 0.1786; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  m=400 rounds x r=400 repeats, A deterministic (coins filled in): beta/L = 0.4024; mean certified bound = 0.4810; bound violated in 0/4000 trials (allowed: 0.1)
  The estimate also has a lower side: mean_i TV - slack <= beta/L with the same probability, so a faithful A is recognised.

== Part D: 'whenever it is issued it is false with probability less than delta' ==
  m=100, delta=0.05: the certificate says rho <= 0.02996.
    a fixed imitator with rho = 1.01 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 4.63e-02 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    a fixed imitator with rho = 2.0 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 2.07e-03 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    a fixed imitator with rho = 5.0 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 8.97e-08 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    if the frozen imitator is good (rho = 0) with probability 0.5 and has rho = 2 x threshold otherwise: Pr[issued and false] = 1.04e-03, Pr[false | issued] = 0.002
    if the frozen imitator is good (rho = 0) with probability 0.01 and has rho = 2 x threshold otherwise: Pr[issued and false] = 2.05e-03, Pr[false | issued] = 0.170
  m=1000, delta=0.05: the certificate says rho <= 0.00300.
    a fixed imitator with rho = 1.01 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 4.83e-02 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    a fixed imitator with rho = 2.0 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 2.46e-03 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    a fixed imitator with rho = 5.0 x threshold: certificate issued with probability 2.79e-07 (< delta: True); given that it is issued it is false with probability 1
    if the frozen imitator is good (rho = 0) with probability 0.5 and has rho = 2 x threshold otherwise: Pr[issued and false] = 1.23e-03, Pr[false | issued] = 0.002
    if the frozen imitator is good (rho = 0) with probability 0.01 and has rho = 2 x threshold otherwise: Pr[issued and false] = 2.43e-03, Pr[false | issued] = 0.196
  The theorem bounds Pr[issued and false] by delta for every imitator; it does not bound Pr[false | issued].
  Repeating a failed validation of the same imitator r times multiplies the bound by r (union bound).
